      "text": "To find the maximum possible area of an equilateral triangle that can fit within a rectangle \\(ABCD\\) with side lengths 10 and 11, we need to consider the constraints imposed by the dimensions of the rectangle. The largest equilateral triangle that can fit within the rectangle will have its sides parallel to the sides of the rectangle, and any internal point of the triangle will still lie within the rectangle.\n\nThe side length \\(s\\) of the equilateral triangle can be determined by the intersection of the triangle's height with the rectangle's sides. If we draw a line from one of the top vertices of the rectangle to the opposite base, this line will intersect the triangle at its midpoint. This midpoint lies on the height of the triangle, and its distance from the base (which is 10 units long) is half the height of the equilateral triangle.\n\nThe height \\(h\\) of an equilateral triangle with side length \\(s\\) is given by:\n\\[\nh = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s\n\\]\nSince the midpoint of the triangle's height is 5 units above the base of the triangle (because the distance from the top of the rectangle to the midpoint of the triangle's height is 5 units), we can set up the equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s = 10 - 5 = 5\n\\]\nSolving for \\(s\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s = 5 \\implies s = \\frac{10}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{10\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\]\n\nThe area \\(A\\) of an equilateral triangle with side length \\(s\\) is given by:\n\\[\nA = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} s^2\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(s = \\frac{10\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\) into the area formula, we get:\n\\[\nA = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\left( \\frac{10\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\right)^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{100 \\cdot 3}{9} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{300}{9} = \\frac{300\\sqrt{3}}{36} = \\frac{25\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the maximum possible area of the equilateral triangle is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{25\\sqrt{3}}{3}}\n\\]",
