      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the maximum possible area of an equilateral triangle that can be inscribed within a rectangle with side lengths \\(10\\) and \\(11\\), we need to consider the geometric constraints of the problem. An equilateral triangle is defined by its side length, so the key is to determine the maximum side length of an equilateral triangle that can fit within the rectangle.\n\nFirst, let's denote the side length of the equilateral triangle as \\(s\\). For the triangle to fit entirely within the rectangle, one of its vertices must lie on a side of the rectangle, and the other two vertices must extend to the opposite side of the rectangle. This configuration imposes the following constraints:\n- The base of the triangle (which is one side of the equilateral triangle) must be parallel to one of the sides of the rectangle.\n- The other two vertices of the triangle must be positioned such that they do not exceed the boundaries of the rectangle.\n\nThere are four possible orientations for the equilateral triangle:\n1. Base on \\(AB\\) or \\(AD\\), with the third vertex on \\(CD\\) or \\(BC\\).\n2. Base on \\(AD\\) or \\(AB\\) with the third vertex on \\(BC\\) or \\(CD\\).\n\nWe will consider the case where the base of the triangle is on \\(AD\\) and the third vertex is on \\(BC\\). The base of the triangle is \\(s\\), and the height of the triangle is \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s\\).\n\nTo find the maximum possible side length \\(s\\), we need to ensure that the height of the triangle does not exceed the height of the rectangle. The height of the rectangle is 11, so we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} s \\leq 11\n\\]\nSolving for \\(s\\), we get:\n\\[\ns \\leq \\frac{2 \\cdot 11}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{22}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{22\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\]\nTherefore, the maximum possible side length of the equilateral triangle is \\(\\frac{22\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\).\n\nThe area \\(A\\) of an equilateral triangle with side length \\(s\\) is given by the formula:\n\\[\nA = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} s^2\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(s = \\frac{22\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\) into the area formula, we get:\n\\[\nA = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\left( \\frac{22\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\right)^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{484 \\cdot 3}{9} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1452}{9} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3} \\cdot 1452}{36} = \\frac{1452\\sqrt{3}}{36} = \\frac{121\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\]\nThus, the maximum possible area of such a triangle is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{121\\sqrt{3}}{3}}\n\\]",
